exec(open(__file__.rsplit("/",1)[0] + "/_h.py").read())
B("Romans", """
1|Paul greets the Christians in Rome and says he wants to visit. The gospel is the power of God for salvation to everyone who believes. Humanity has turned from God to idols, and God has given them over to their sins.
2|God judges everyone fairly, Jews and Gentiles alike. Having the law does not help unless you do it. True circumcision is of the heart.
3|All have sinned and fall short of the glory of God. People are justified by faith in Jesus Christ, not by works of the law. God's faithfulness is not canceled by human unfaithfulness.
4|Abraham was counted righteous because he believed God, before he was circumcised. He is the father of all who believe.
5|Having been justified by faith, we have peace with God. Christ died for us while we were still sinners. As sin came through Adam, grace comes through Christ.
6|Baptized into Christ's death, we are to walk in newness of life. Do not let sin reign in your body. The wages of sin is death, but the gift of God is eternal life.
7|Paul describes the struggle between wanting to do good and doing wrong. Who will rescue me from this body of death? Thanks be to God through Jesus Christ.
8|There is no condemnation for those in Christ Jesus. Those led by the Spirit are children of God. Nothing can separate us from the love of God in Christ Jesus.
9|Paul is sorrowful about his fellow Israelites. God chooses by mercy, not by human effort. God is the potter, and the Gentiles are called as well.
10|Israel pursued the law but did not attain righteousness. If you confess with your mouth that Jesus is Lord and believe in your heart that God raised him, you will be saved. How beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news.
11|God has not rejected his people. There is a remnant chosen by grace. The Gentiles are grafted in, and all Israel will be saved. O the depth of the riches of God's wisdom.
12|Present your bodies as a living sacrifice. Do not be conformed to the world but be transformed by renewing your mind. Use your gifts. Love sincerely, and overcome evil with good.
13|Be subject to governing authorities. Love your neighbor and fulfill the law. Put on the Lord Jesus Christ. The day is near.
14|Do not judge one another over food or special days. Do not cause a weaker brother to stumble. The kingdom of God is righteousness, peace and joy in the Holy Spirit.
15|Bear with the weak, as Christ did. Paul explains his mission to the Gentiles and plans to visit Rome and Spain, after delivering a collection to Jerusalem.
16|Paul sends greetings to many by name, including Phoebe, Priscilla and Aquila. He warns about those who cause divisions. He ends with a doxology.
""")
B("1 Corinthians", """
1|Paul greets the church in Corinth. He urges them to be united and says there should be no divisions. The message of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us it is the power of God.
2|Paul did not come with eloquence but with the power of the Spirit. God has revealed his wisdom to us through the Spirit.
3|The Corinthians are still infants in Christ, fighting over leaders. Paul planted, Apollos watered, but God gave the growth. You are God's temple.
4|Apostles are servants of Christ and stewards of God's mysteries. Paul says he is a father to them and will come to them soon.
5|Paul tells the church to remove a man living in sexual immorality. A little yeast leavens the whole batch.
6|Christians should not take each other to court. Do you not know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit? Flee from sexual immorality.
7|Paul answers questions about marriage and celibacy. Each person should live as God has called them. The time is short.
8|Food offered to idols: knowledge puffs up but love builds up. Do not make a weaker believer stumble.
9|Paul gave up his rights for the gospel. He becomes all things to all people. Like an athlete, he disciplines his body to win a crown that lasts.
10|Israel's failures in the wilderness are a warning. God will not let you be tempted beyond what you can bear. Do all for the glory of God. We who are many are one body because we share one bread.
11|Men and women in worship. The Lord's Supper: Paul passes on what he received, that the Lord Jesus took bread and said, this is my body. Examine yourselves before eating.
12|Spiritual gifts: there are different gifts but the same Spirit. The church is one body with many parts.
13|If I have all gifts but do not have love, I am nothing. Love is patient and kind. Faith, hope and love remain, and the greatest of these is love.
14|Prophecy is better than tongues in the assembly, because it builds up the church. Everything should be done in order.
15|The gospel Paul received: Christ died for our sins, was buried, and rose on the third day. If Christ has not been raised, our faith is futile. The dead will be raised imperishable. Death is swallowed up in victory.
16|The collection for the saints in Jerusalem. Paul's travel plans. Stand firm in the faith. Let all that you do be done in love.
""")
B("2 Corinthians", """
1|Paul praises the God of all comfort, who comforts us in our troubles. He explains why he changed his travel plans.
2|Paul did not want to cause the Corinthians pain. He asks them to forgive and comfort the one who was disciplined. We are the aroma of Christ.
3|The Corinthians are Paul's letter, written on hearts. The old covenant brought death, the new covenant brings life through the Spirit. We are being transformed into his image.
4|We have this treasure in jars of clay. We are afflicted but not crushed. Our light and momentary troubles are achieving an eternal glory.
5|If our earthly tent is destroyed, we have a building from God. We live by faith, not by sight. The love of Christ compels us. If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation, and we are ambassadors for Christ.
6|Now is the day of salvation. Paul lists his hardships. Do not be yoked with unbelievers.
7|Paul is comforted by Titus's news. Godly sorrow leads to repentance, and worldly sorrow leads to death.
8|The churches of Macedonia gave generously out of their poverty. Paul urges the Corinthians to finish their collection. Jesus became poor so that we could become rich.
9|God loves a cheerful giver. He who sows generously will reap generously.
10|Paul defends his authority. The weapons we fight with are not the weapons of the world. Let the one who boasts boast in the Lord.
11|Paul speaks as a fool and compares himself with the false apostles. He lists his sufferings and shows the dangers he faced.
12|Paul speaks of a man caught up to the third heaven. He has a thorn in the flesh, and the Lord says: my grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.
13|Paul warns them that he is coming a third time. Examine yourselves. He closes with a farewell and a blessing: the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ and the love of God and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be with you all.
""")
B("Galatians", """
1|Paul is amazed that the Galatians are turning to a different gospel. He received his gospel through a revelation of Jesus Christ, not from men. He tells of his conversion.
2|Paul meets the apostles in Jerusalem, who accept his gospel. He confronts Peter in Antioch. We are justified by faith in Christ and not by works of the law. I have been crucified with Christ.
3|You foolish Galatians! Did you receive the Spirit by works of the law, or by believing? Abraham believed. Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law. In Christ there is neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female.
4|We are no longer slaves but children and heirs. Paul fears for the Galatians. The story of Hagar and Sarah as an allegory of two covenants.
5|For freedom Christ has set us free. Faith works through love. The works of the flesh are listed, and the fruit of the Spirit: love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control.
6|Bear one another's burdens. Whatever a person sows, that will he also reap. Paul closes in his own hand: far be it from me to boast except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ.
""")
B("Ephesians", """
1|God has blessed us in Christ with every spiritual blessing. He chose us before the creation of the world and adopted us as his children. Paul prays that they will know the hope to which God has called them.
2|We were dead in our sins, but God made us alive in Christ. By grace you have been saved through faith. Jews and Gentiles are made one in Christ, who is our peace.
3|Paul explains the mystery that the Gentiles are fellow heirs. He prays that they would know the love of Christ that surpasses knowledge.
4|Live a life worthy of your calling. There is one body, one Spirit, one hope, one Lord, one faith, one baptism, one God. Put off the old self and put on the new. Be kind and forgive one another.
5|Walk in love as Christ loved us. Do not get drunk on wine. Wives and husbands: a husband should love his wife as Christ loved the church.
6|Children obey your parents. Fathers do not provoke your children. Servants and masters. Put on the whole armor of God, to stand against the devil's schemes.
""")
B("Philippians", """
1|Paul, in prison, thanks God for the Philippians and prays that their love will abound. To live is Christ and to die is gain. He asks them to live in a manner worthy of the gospel.
2|Have the same mind as Christ Jesus, who humbled himself and became obedient to death on a cross. Every knee will bow at the name of Jesus. Shine like stars in the world. Paul commends Timothy and Epaphroditus.
3|Paul warns against those who trust in the flesh. He says everything he once gained he now counts as loss for knowing Christ. I press on toward the goal for the prize of the heavenly call.
4|Rejoice in the Lord always. Do not be anxious about anything. Whatever is true, honorable, just, pure, lovely and commendable, think about these things. I can do all things through him who strengthens me.
""")
B("Colossians", """
1|Paul thanks God for the Colossians' faith and love. Christ is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation. In him all things hold together, and through him God reconciled all things.
2|Paul wants them to be encouraged and to know Christ, in whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom. See that no one takes you captive by empty philosophy. You were buried with him in baptism and raised with him.
3|Set your minds on things above. Put to death what belongs to your earthly nature. Put on compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness and patience. Above all, put on love. Rules for households.
4|Devote yourselves to prayer. Be wise in how you act toward outsiders. Paul sends greetings from friends, including Luke the doctor.
""")
B("1 Thessalonians", """
1|Paul thanks God for the Thessalonians' work of faith, labor of love and steadfast hope. They turned from idols to serve the living God and wait for his Son from heaven.
2|Paul reminds them how he and his companions lived among them like a nurse caring for her children. They accepted the message as the word of God. Paul longs to see them again.
3|Timothy returns with good news about their faith and love. Paul prays that the Lord will make their love increase and overflow.
4|Live to please God and be holy. Love one another. Do not grieve like those without hope: the dead in Christ will rise first, and we will be with the Lord forever.
5|The day of the Lord will come like a thief in the night. Be alert and sober. Encourage one another. Rejoice always, pray continually, give thanks in all circumstances.
""")
B("2 Thessalonians", """
1|Paul thanks God for their faith and endurance in persecution. When the Lord Jesus is revealed from heaven, he will bring justice to those who trouble them and relief to those who suffer.
2|Do not be shaken by claims that the day of the Lord has already come. The man of lawlessness must come first. Stand firm and hold to the teachings you were given.
3|Pray for us. Keep away from those who live idly. If anyone is unwilling to work, let him not eat. Do not grow weary in doing good.
""")
B("1 Timothy", """
1|Paul urges Timothy to stay in Ephesus and stop false teaching. The aim of the commandment is love. Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, of whom I am the foremost.
2|Prayers should be made for everyone, including kings. There is one God and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus. Instructions about men and women in worship.
3|Qualifications for overseers and deacons. The church is the pillar and foundation of the truth. Great is the mystery of godliness.
4|In later times some will depart from the faith. Everything created by God is good. Timothy should be an example to believers and not neglect his gift.
5|Instructions about caring for widows, elders and the family. Do not be hasty in appointing leaders. Use a little wine for your stomach.
6|Servants should honor their masters. Godliness with contentment is great gain. The love of money is a root of all kinds of evil. Fight the good fight of the faith.
""")
B("2 Timothy", """
1|Paul, in prison, thanks God for Timothy's faith, and tells him to fan into flame the gift of God. God gave us a spirit not of fear but of power, love and self-control. Do not be ashamed of the gospel.
2|Be strong in the grace that is in Christ Jesus. Endure hardship like a good soldier. Present yourself to God as a worker who correctly handles the word of truth. Flee youthful passions.
3|In the last days people will be lovers of self and of money. All Scripture is breathed out by God and is useful for teaching, reproof, correction and training in righteousness.
4|Preach the word in season and out of season. Paul says: I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith. He asks Timothy to come soon and bring Mark and his cloak.
""")
B("Titus", """
1|Paul left Titus in Crete to appoint elders. He lists the qualifications for an overseer. The Cretans need to be corrected, as some are false teachers.
2|Teach sound doctrine to older men, older women, young women, young men and slaves. The grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation for all people.
3|Remind people to be submissive to rulers and to be ready for every good work. We were once foolish, but God saved us by his mercy through the washing of regeneration. Avoid foolish controversies.
""")
B("Philemon", """
1|Paul writes to Philemon about Onesimus, a runaway slave who has become a Christian. Paul sends him back and asks Philemon to receive him no longer as a slave but as a beloved brother.
""")
B("Hebrews", """
1|God spoke in the past through the prophets, but now through his Son, who is the radiance of God's glory. The Son is greater than the angels.
2|We must pay attention to what we have heard. Jesus was made lower than the angels for a little while, tasted death for everyone, and shares our flesh and blood to free us from fear of death.
3|Jesus is greater than Moses, as a son over God's house. Do not harden your hearts as Israel did in the wilderness.
4|There remains a Sabbath rest for God's people. The word of God is living and active. We have a great high priest, Jesus, who sympathizes with our weakness. Let us approach the throne of grace with confidence.
5|Every high priest is appointed to offer sacrifices. Christ is a priest forever in the order of Melchizedek. He learned obedience through suffering. The readers are told to grow up in their understanding.
6|Let us move on toward maturity. It is impossible to restore those who fall away. God's promise to Abraham is an anchor of the soul, sure and steadfast.
7|Melchizedek, king of Salem, blessed Abraham. Jesus is a priest forever in his order, and he always lives to intercede for us.
8|Jesus is the high priest of a better covenant. God promises: I will put my laws into their minds and write them on their hearts. The old covenant is becoming obsolete.
9|The old covenant's tabernacle and rituals. Christ entered the heavenly sanctuary with his own blood, which cleanses our conscience. He will appear a second time to save those who are waiting for him.
10|The law is only a shadow of good things to come. By one offering Christ has perfected for all time those who are being sanctified. Let us hold fast the confession of our hope. Do not throw away your confidence.
11|Faith is the assurance of things hoped for. By faith Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Moses, Rahab and many others pleased God.
12|Since we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, let us run with endurance, looking to Jesus. God disciplines those he loves. Pursue peace and holiness. We receive a kingdom that cannot be shaken.
13|Keep loving each other. Show hospitality to strangers. Remember those in prison. Jesus Christ is the same yesterday and today and forever. Obey your leaders. Let us offer a sacrifice of praise.
""")
B("James", """
1|Count it all joy when you face trials. If you lack wisdom, ask God. Blessed is the one who endures. Be doers of the word, and not hearers only. Pure religion is to look after orphans and widows.
2|Do not show favoritism to the rich. Faith without works is dead. Abraham and Rahab were shown righteous by their works.
3|The tongue is a small member but boasts great things. No one can tame it. Wisdom from above is pure, peaceable and gentle.
4|Quarrels come from your desires. Submit yourselves to God. Resist the devil and he will flee from you. Draw near to God and he will draw near to you. Do not boast about tomorrow.
5|Woe to rich oppressors who hold back wages. Be patient until the Lord comes. Is anyone sick? Let him call the elders of the church to pray over him and anoint him with oil. The prayer of a righteous person is powerful.
""")
B("1 Peter", """
1|Peter writes to Christians scattered in Asia Minor. God has given us a new birth into a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus. You were ransomed with the precious blood of Christ. Be holy as God is holy.
2|Like living stones, you are being built into a spiritual house, a chosen people and a royal priesthood. Submit to every human authority. Christ suffered for you, leaving you an example.
3|Wives and husbands. Do not repay evil for evil. Always be ready to give an answer for the hope that is in you. Christ died once for sins. Baptism now saves you.
4|Arm yourselves with the same attitude as Christ. Love covers a multitude of sins. If you suffer as a Christian, do not be ashamed.
5|Elders should shepherd the flock willingly. Clothe yourselves with humility. Cast all your anxieties on him, because he cares for you. Be sober, be watchful. Your adversary the devil prowls around like a roaring lion.
""")
B("2 Peter", """
1|Peter reminds his readers to grow in faith, virtue, knowledge, self-control, steadfastness, godliness, brotherly affection and love. He says he is about to die. No prophecy of Scripture comes from private interpretation, for men spoke from God as they were moved by the Holy Spirit.
2|There will be false teachers who bring destructive heresies. God punished the angels who sinned, spared Noah and rescued Lot. The false teachers are like springs without water.
3|Scoffers will ask: where is the promise of his coming? With the Lord one day is like a thousand years. The Lord is patient, not wanting any to perish. The day of the Lord will come like a thief. Grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord.
""")
B("1 John", """
1|John writes about what he has seen, heard and touched, the Word of life. God is light, and in him there is no darkness. If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us.
2|Jesus Christ the righteous is our advocate with the Father. Whoever keeps his commandments knows him. Do not love the world. Many antichrists have come. Remain in him.
3|See what kind of love the Father has given us, that we should be called children of God. Whoever is born of God does not keep on sinning. We should love one another in deed and in truth.
4|Test the spirits to see whether they are from God. God is love. We love because he first loved us. There is no fear in love.
5|Whoever believes that Jesus is the Christ is born of God. This is the victory that overcomes the world, our faith. God has given us eternal life, and this life is in his Son.
""")
B("2 John", """
1|John writes to the elect lady and her children. He says to walk in truth and love one another. Do not welcome those who deny that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh.
""")
B("3 John", """
1|John writes to Gaius, who is walking in the truth and helps traveling missionaries. Diotrephes, who likes to be first, will not welcome the brothers. Demetrius has a good reputation.
""")
B("Jude", """
1|Jude urges his readers to contend for the faith that was once for all delivered to the saints. He warns of false teachers, using examples from Israel, angels, Sodom, Cain and Enoch. He ends with a praise: to him who is able to keep you from stumbling.
""")
B("Revelation", """
1|John, on the island of Patmos, hears a loud voice and sees one like a son of man among seven lampstands. He says: I am the first and the last, and I have the keys of death and Hades. John is told to write what he sees.
2|Letters to the churches in Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum and Thyatira. Each is praised or warned, and given a promise to those who conquer.
3|Letters to the churches in Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea. Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, I will come in.
4|John sees a door open in heaven and a throne with someone seated on it. Twenty-four elders and four living creatures worship God day and night: holy, holy, holy.
5|A scroll with seven seals can be opened only by the Lamb who was slain. The living creatures and elders sing a new song, and countless angels join in praise.
6|The Lamb opens the first six seals: four horsemen (conquest, war, famine, death), martyrs crying for justice, and a great earthquake.
7|144,000 are sealed from the tribes of Israel. A great multitude from every nation stands before the throne in white robes, washed in the blood of the Lamb.
8|The seventh seal is opened and there is silence in heaven. Seven angels receive trumpets. The first four trumpets strike a third of the land, sea, rivers and sky.
9|The fifth trumpet unleashes locusts from the abyss. The sixth trumpet releases an army of four angels. The people still do not repent.
10|A mighty angel with a little scroll. John is told to eat it. It is sweet in his mouth and bitter in his stomach.
11|The two witnesses prophesy for 1,260 days, are killed by the beast, and rise after three and a half days. The seventh trumpet sounds: the kingdom of the world has become the kingdom of our Lord.
12|A woman clothed with the sun gives birth to a son and a great red dragon tries to devour him. War in heaven: Michael and his angels defeat the dragon. The woman is protected in the wilderness.
13|A beast rises from the sea and another from the earth. The beast's number is 666. People are forced to worship the beast.
14|The Lamb stands on Mount Zion with 144,000. Three angels proclaim the gospel, the fall of Babylon and a warning. The harvest and the winepress of God's wrath.
15|Seven angels with the last seven plagues. Those who had conquered the beast sing the song of Moses and the Lamb.
16|The seven bowls of God's wrath are poured out. The sixth dries the Euphrates. Armageddon. The seventh brings an earthquake and great hail. It is done.
17|The great prostitute, Babylon, rides a scarlet beast. The beast and ten kings fight the Lamb, but the Lamb conquers them.
18|Babylon falls. Kings and merchants mourn. A mighty angel throws a millstone into the sea. Heaven rejoices.
19|Heaven rejoices because the marriage of the Lamb has come. A rider on a white horse called Faithful and True defeats the beast and the false prophet.
20|Satan is bound for a thousand years. The martyrs reign with Christ. Satan is released and defeated. The great white throne judgment: the dead are judged. Death and Hades are thrown into the lake of fire.
21|A new heaven and a new earth. The holy city, the new Jerusalem, comes down from God. God will wipe away every tear. There is no more death. The city has twelve gates and twelve foundations and needs no sun.
22|The river of the water of life flows from the throne, and the tree of life bears fruit each month. They will see his face. Jesus says: I am coming soon. The Spirit and the Bride say: Come. Come, Lord Jesus.
""")
